\section{Over half the grid is already gone}{

Lines with both ends in one NUTS3 region are dropped, exactly as
\code{pypsa.clustering.spatial.aggregatelines()} drops intra-cluster lines. That
is \strong{4,218 of 7,360 lines (57\%), carrying 53\% of capacity} — discarded at
the \emph{finest} level available. Coarsening further only removes more.
}

\section{Aggregating correctly}{

Three different rules, none of them a plain average:
\itemize{
\item \code{s_nom}, \code{num_parallel} — \strong{sum};
\item \code{length} — \strong{recomputed} as the great-circle distance between the two
region centroids times \code{line_length_factor} (1.25). It is not inherited, and
it \emph{grows}: median corridor length rises from 85 km at NUTS3 to 459 km at
NUTS0;
\item \code{x}, \code{r} — rescaled by the length ratio, then \strong{parallel-combined} as
\code{1 / sum(1/x)}.
}

Because impedance follows length, losses are \strong{overestimated} at coarse
levels; because \code{s_nom} is a plain thermal sum ignoring N-1 and loop flows,
capacity is \strong{overestimated} too. The two do not cancel.
}

